    "case_id": "457",
    "case_summary": "A 26-year-old man presented with a several-year history of persistent round lesions with a scar-like appearance on his face. His condition had previously been diagnosed and treated unsuccessfully. He denied experiencing previous inflammation and had no history of chicken pox, acne or traumatic lesions in those areas.\n\nSeveral round atrophic lesions on his face, symmetric and bilateral, located especially on the malar and preauricular areas, with a diameter of 5 to 10 mm. The skin lesions were not indurated and displayed no epidermal changes.\n\n- Histopathologic examination:     - Showed significant epidermal dell and superficial dermal lymphocytic infiltrate associated with dermal fibrosis    - Orcein stain demonstrated reduction of elastic fibers in the dermis beneath the epidermal dell  Images:  - Figure 1:    Title: Atrophic lesions on the patient    Description: Shows atrophic lesions on the patient's left cheek (A) and with a cicatricial appearance on the preauricular right area (B)    - Figure 2:    Title: Histological examination    Description: A) Panoramic histological view shows slight depression of the epidermis (hematoxylin-eosin; original magnification, ×2)    B) Orcein staining shows reduction in elastic fibers beside lymphocytic perivascular infiltrate compared with surrounding healthy area (orcein; original magnification, ×10)",
